1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,013
A data centre load balancer is moving to the

2
00:00:04,013 --> 00:00:06,250
cloud. The cut-over is on Saturday.

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:09,574
The configuration on the new platform was rebuilt by

4
00:00:09,574 --> 00:00:12,643
hand from a spreadsheet, and nobody can say with

5
00:00:12,643 --> 00:00:14,880
certainty that the two sides match.

6
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:17,687
What if Claude read both sides,

7
00:00:17,687 --> 00:00:20,872
object by object, told you exactly what differs,

8
00:00:20,872 --> 00:00:23,659
why it matters, and what to fix before the

9
00:00:23,659 --> 00:00:27,242
change window opens? That is configuration comparison,

10
00:00:27,242 --> 00:00:30,294
automated with Claude, and governed by people.

11
00:00:31,044 --> 00:00:35,724
Gennoor Tech presents Configuration Comparison Automation with Claude.

12
00:00:36,474 --> 00:00:39,692
Two platforms. One verified difference report.

13
00:00:39,692 --> 00:00:42,070
Cloud to cloud, physical to cloud,

14
00:00:42,070 --> 00:00:43,889
or cloud back to physical,

15
00:00:43,889 --> 00:00:46,057
the problem is always the same:

16
00:00:46,057 --> 00:00:50,464
two configurations written in different dialects that must mean

17
00:00:50,464 --> 00:00:53,612
the same thing. You build the pipeline in ten

18
00:00:53,612 --> 00:00:56,620
modules. Claude plans, drafts and explains.

19
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,418
Deterministic Python collects and diffs.

20
00:00:59,418 --> 00:01:02,565
A person approves every consequential action.

21
00:01:02,565 --> 00:01:04,314
And everything is logged.

22
00:01:05,064 --> 00:01:07,623
You start with the planning discipline:

23
00:01:07,623 --> 00:01:10,183
what a migration comparison must prove,

24
00:01:10,183 --> 00:01:13,135
which objects matter, and why a raw text diff

25
00:01:13,135 --> 00:01:16,220
between an F five and a NetScaler is worthless.

26
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,188
Then you set up the workbench.

27
00:01:18,188 --> 00:01:20,354
Claude Code for interactive work,

28
00:01:20,354 --> 00:01:22,979
the Claude Agent S D K for the pipeline,

29
00:01:22,979 --> 00:01:26,260
and M C P servers that expose read-only collectors

30
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,016
as tools Claude can call but never bypass.

31
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:33,336
Day one ends with collection and normalisation.

32
00:01:33,336 --> 00:01:37,818
You pull running configuration from F five through iControl

33
00:01:37,818 --> 00:01:39,338
R E S T and t m s h,

34
00:01:39,338 --> 00:01:42,452
from Citrix NetScaler through the N I T R

35
00:01:42,452 --> 00:01:44,655
O A P I, from Radware Alteon,

36
00:01:44,655 --> 00:01:47,998
from other load balancers such as Anaya L B,

37
00:01:47,998 --> 00:01:50,732
and from Cisco A P I C and Terraform

38
00:01:50,732 --> 00:01:53,315
state. Every virtual server, pool,

39
00:01:53,315 --> 00:01:58,101
monitor, certificate and policy lands in one canonical Pydantic

40
00:01:58,101 --> 00:02:02,431
model, with provenance on every field and unknowns marked

41
00:02:02,431 --> 00:02:04,710
explicitly instead of guessed.

42
00:02:05,460 --> 00:02:07,103
On day two Claude plans.

43
00:02:07,103 --> 00:02:10,593
Given the source inventory and the target platform,

44
00:02:10,593 --> 00:02:13,742
it drafts the migration plan as structured J S

45
00:02:13,742 --> 00:02:16,138
O N: object mappings, feature gaps,

46
00:02:16,138 --> 00:02:19,834
dependency order and risks. It must cite the inventory

47
00:02:19,834 --> 00:02:22,708
it was given, and it must say plainly what

48
00:02:22,708 --> 00:02:26,336
cannot be mapped. Each plan step carries a confidence

49
00:02:26,336 --> 00:02:29,622
and an owner, so the review meeting argues about

50
00:02:29,622 --> 00:02:33,318
facts, not formatting. You test it with incomplete and

51
00:02:33,318 --> 00:02:36,808
contradictory input and watch it refuse to invent a

52
00:02:36,808 --> 00:02:37,356
mapping.

53
00:02:38,106 --> 00:02:42,350
Then the diff engine. Deterministic code compares canonical models

54
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:45,886
field by field, classifies each difference by severity,

55
00:02:45,886 --> 00:02:48,394
and produces a machine-readable report.

56
00:02:48,394 --> 00:02:50,387
Claude never computes the diff.

57
00:02:50,387 --> 00:02:53,280
It reads the diff through tool use and writes

58
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:57,138
the explanation, the drift summary and the remediation steps

59
00:02:57,138 --> 00:02:58,617
an engineer can act on,

60
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:01,125
with every claim tied to a report line.

61
00:03:01,125 --> 00:03:04,532
You will seed a hidden persistence mismatch and watch

62
00:03:04,532 --> 00:03:05,754
the engine find it.

63
00:03:06,504 --> 00:03:10,847
Day three is governance. Collectors run with read-only credentials.

64
00:03:10,847 --> 00:03:14,153
Every report becomes a GitHub pull request with the

65
00:03:14,153 --> 00:03:18,366
evidence attached, and a ServiceNow change record created through

66
00:03:18,366 --> 00:03:20,894
the Table A P I and routed for approval

67
00:03:20,894 --> 00:03:24,265
in Flow Designer. Nothing is applied to any platform

68
00:03:24,265 --> 00:03:27,635
without a named approver, and the approval itself is

69
00:03:27,635 --> 00:03:29,256
part of the audit record.

70
00:03:30,006 --> 00:03:32,477
You then wire it into GitHub Actions:

71
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:36,685
Terraform plan output compared against live state before apply,

72
00:03:36,685 --> 00:03:39,423
scheduled drift checks between platforms,

73
00:03:39,423 --> 00:03:42,428
and an audit trail that records every prompt,

74
00:03:42,428 --> 00:03:44,766
every tool call and every decision,

75
00:03:44,766 --> 00:03:47,838
so a reviewer can replay the run months later.

76
00:03:48,588 --> 00:03:51,654
Every lab runs against lab devices and recorded A

77
00:03:51,654 --> 00:03:54,533
P I responses, so you can break things safely.

78
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:57,099
You will compare a physical F five with a

79
00:03:57,099 --> 00:04:01,104
cloud NetScaler, seed a hidden persistence timeout mismatch into

80
00:04:01,104 --> 00:04:04,170
the target, and prove the diff engine catches it,

81
00:04:04,170 --> 00:04:07,048
Claude explains it, and the tests never let it

82
00:04:07,048 --> 00:04:10,803
slip through. Then you attempt an unapproved remediation and

83
00:04:10,803 --> 00:04:13,932
prove the pipeline blocks it and logs the attempt.

84
00:04:14,682 --> 00:04:17,127
Here is the full table of contents,

85
00:04:17,127 --> 00:04:19,013
10 modules over three days.

86
00:04:19,013 --> 00:04:23,204
Day one: Configuration comparison as a migration discipline;

87
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:26,347
Setting up Claude Code, the Agent SDK and MCP

88
00:04:26,347 --> 00:04:30,747
for the workflow; Collecting configuration from every platform;

89
00:04:30,747 --> 00:04:34,449
and Normalising into a canonical configuration model.

90
00:04:34,449 --> 00:04:37,243
Day two: Migration planning with Claude;

91
00:04:37,243 --> 00:04:41,923
The deterministic diff engine; and Claude explains the differences.

92
00:04:41,923 --> 00:04:46,952
Day three: Guardrails, approvals and audit for configuration comparison;

93
00:04:46,952 --> 00:04:50,654
Scheduling, Terraform plan checks and GitHub Actions;

94
00:04:50,654 --> 00:04:54,426
and Capstone: end-to-end physical-to-cloud comparison.

95
00:04:55,176 --> 00:04:59,348
Every build in this course follows six engineering principles.

96
00:04:59,348 --> 00:05:01,164
Model output is a proposal,

97
00:05:01,164 --> 00:05:04,663
never a fact. Authorization lives outside the model.

98
00:05:04,663 --> 00:05:07,623
A human approves every consequential action.

99
00:05:07,623 --> 00:05:09,911
Decisions and evidence are logged.

100
00:05:09,911 --> 00:05:13,881
The stack stays provider-agnostic. And quality is measured,

101
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:14,688
not assumed.

102
00:05:15,438 --> 00:05:19,712
Three migration directions. Five platform A P I families.

103
00:05:19,712 --> 00:05:22,561
One canonical schema for every object.

104
00:05:22,561 --> 00:05:25,710
And zero changes applied without approval.

105
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:29,530
This course is for enterprise I T and network

106
00:05:29,530 --> 00:05:33,760
operations teams, load balancer and cloud migration engineers,

107
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:36,830
and automation leads, in India and worldwide.

108
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:40,786
You need basic networking, Python and command line skills.

109
00:05:40,786 --> 00:05:42,901
Prior use of Claude is helpful,

110
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:43,788
not required.

111
00:05:44,538 --> 00:05:48,080
Know every difference before the change window opens.

112
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:51,288
Configuration Comparison Automation with Claude.

113
00:05:51,288 --> 00:05:53,226
Enrol now at gennoor dot com.
